  <section id="judge" class="container-custom max-w-6xl py-10 md:py-16 bg-cream-dark rounded-3xl">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-text-primary mb-4">How to judge any diet in seven questions</h2>
    <p class="text-lg text-text-secondary leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mb-8">
      Every plan I have ever handed a client had to survive these seven questions first. They are also the exact checklist behind each guide above. It takes about two minutes to apply to any plan a book, influencer, GP or app will ever hand you — and if a plan survives all seven, it will probably work, whatever its name is.
    </p>
    <ol class="space-y-4 max-w-3xl">
      {[
        ['Does it run on whole food?', 'Look at the actual ingredient stream, not the marketing. If 80% of it is recognisably food, good start. If it requires branded bars, shakes or powders, that is a product business wearing a diet costume.'],
        ['Does it cover protein?', 'Around 0.8 g/kg body weight minimum for healthy adults — higher for older adults, hard training or a calorie deficit. Any diet that cannot get you there is a weight plan first and a health plan second.'],
        ['Does it cover fibre and micronutrients?', 'Where the plants, whole grains, nuts, fruit and legumes sit in the plan. Total elimination of a whole food family needs a reason and usually a supplement.'],
        ['Is the energy balance survivable?', 'Portions should land near what you burn. If the plan requires misery to maintain a deficit, you will regain and blame yourself rather than the plan.'],
        ['What are you being asked to eliminate, and why?', 'Ultra-processed food earns elimination with the most consistent evidence in nutrition. Food families like grains, dairy or plants get no such high ground — legitimate arguments exist, but the burden of proof is on the eliminator.'],
        ['What is the evidence grade on the actual claim?', 'Not anecdotes. Not testimonials. Randomized trials, cohorts, or better a meta-analysis. This page explains the grades; every guide wears its own on its sleeve.'],
        ['Would you still be doing it in five years?', 'The most underused question in nutrition. Over twelve-month horizons nearly every sensible plan converges; over five years, only the sustainable ones have members.']
      ].map(([q, a], i) => (
        <li class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-border p-5 shadow-sm">
          <p class="font-bold text-text-primary flex gap-3"><span class="text-sage">{i + 1}.</span> {q}</p>
          <p class="mt-1.5 text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">{a}</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section id="limits" class="container-custom max-w-6xl py-10 md:py-16">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-text-primary mb-4">When a diet is not okay</h2>
    <p class="text-lg text-text-secondary leading-relaxed max-w-3xl mb-8">
      I have also met the people who found the edge of "any sensible diet can work" the hard way — some of them on plans I would have run differently today, back when I had not yet learned where the fence was. So read this section slowly. Some patterns are not sensible as written. Some people should not restrict without a clinician watching. And a few red flags cancel everything else a website claims, whatever credentials stand behind it.
    </p>
    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-5">
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-border bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
        <p class="font-bold text-text-primary mb-2">Red flags in the plan itself</p>
        <ul class="space-y-2.5 text-text-secondary text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-error font-bold" aria-hidden="true">×</span>Bans an entire food group without a medical reason and expects you to buy their supplement line</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-error font-bold" aria-hidden="true">×</span>Claims to work for everyone, always, and dismisses real risks as myths</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-error font-bold" aria-hidden="true">×</span>Sells the "one true diet" and treats every alternative as sabotage or corruption</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-error font-bold" aria-hidden="true">×</span>Promises more than 2 lbs/week of fat loss ad infinitum, or "cures" a chronic disease</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-error font-bold" aria-hidden="true">×</span>Cites no studies, or cites only testimonials, or cannot tell you what its own risks are</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-2xl border border-border bg-white p-6 shadow-sm">
        <p class="font-bold text-text-primary mb-2">The people who should not self-restrict</p>
        <ul class="space-y-2.5 text-text-secondary text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-terracotta-dark font-bold" aria-hidden="true">!</span>Anyone with type 1 diabetes, kidney disease, heart failure, or a complex medication regimen — dietary change can change drug doses</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-terracotta-dark font-bold" aria-hidden="true">!</span>Anyone pregnant or breastfeeding — restriction has a real cost curve</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-terracotta-dark font-bold" aria-hidden="true">!</span>Anyone with a personal or family history of disordered eating; rigid food rules reliably escalate</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-terracotta-dark font-bold" aria-hidden="true">!</span>Children and teenagers, whose nutritional floors are higher and whose relationships with food are still loading</li>
          <li class="flex gap-2"><span class="text-terracotta-dark font-bold" aria-hidden="true">!</span>Older adults under-eating protein — sarcopenia hides in calorie deficits</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-text-primary leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
      Where does that leave the strictest patterns — carnivore, 80/10/10, macrobiotic? We cover them anyway, in full, honestly, with their grade-D evidence in the first screen rather than buried nine scrolls down. Read <a href="/diets/carnivore" class="text-sage-dark hover:text-sage font-medium underline decoration-sage/40 underline-offset-2">the carnivore guide</a> or <a href="/diets/80-10-10" class="text-sage-dark hover:text-sage font-medium underline decoration-sage/40 underline-offset-2">the 80/10/10 guide</a> and you will see the relief-thesis does not need to lie for the extreme versions — it simply cannot stretch that far, and this hub says so.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="method" class="container-custom max-w-6xl py-10 md:py-16 bg-white border-y border-border">
    <div class="grid lg:grid-cols-2 gap-10">
      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-text-primary mb-4">How we research the diet guides</h2>
        <p class="text-text-secondary leading-relaxed mb-4">The gym floor taught me what to look for; this pipeline is how every guide earns its verdict. The outcome grades get published, not buried in the eleventh paragraph.</p>
        <ol class="space-y-3 text-sm text-text-secondary">
          <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-sage font-bold">1</span><span><strong class="text-text-primary">Source the primary literature.</strong> Randomized trials, systematic reviews, large prospective cohorts and meta-analyses — with DOIs, so you can verify every claim in one click.</span></li>
          <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-sage font-bold">2</span><span><strong class="text-text-primary">Grade the evidence</strong> A through D, domain by domain, on a standard rubric adapted from GRADE — not a vibe.</span></li>
          <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-sage font-bold">3</span><span><strong class="text-text-primary">Write for the reader, not the exam.</strong> Plain sentences, real numbers, mechanistic jargon translated on first use.</span></li>
          <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-sage font-bold">4</span><span><strong class="text-text-primary">Separate food from the label.</strong> "Clean" vs "dirty" versions are explicitly addressed for every pattern that has them.</span></li>
          <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-sage font-bold">5</span><span><strong class="text-text-primary">Publish the risks with the verdict.</strong> Every guide carries a "who should not do this" section and a firm not-medical-advice notice.</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-text-primary mb-4">What the evidence grades mean</h3>
        <div class="space-y-3 text-sm">
          {[
            ['A', 'bg-sage-dark', 'Strong', 'Multiple randomized trials or meta-analyses of consistent, meaningful effect; endorsed by major health bodies.'],
            ['B', 'bg-sage', 'Moderate', 'Randomized trials or large cohorts with generally consistent results; moderate effect sizes.'],
            ['C', 'bg-terracotta-dark', 'Limited', 'Small trials, observational data, or inconsistent results. Promising, not proven.'],
            ['D', 'bg-error', 'Insufficient', 'Mainly mechanistic reasoning, case reports and testimonials. No randomized trials.']
          ].map(([letter, bg, label, text]) => (
            <div class="flex gap-4 rounded-2xl border border-border bg-cream-dark p-4">
              <span class:list={[bg, 'w-9 h-9 flex items-center justify-center rounded-full text-white font-bold flex-shrink-0']}>{letter}</span>
              <div>
                <p class="font-bold text-text-primary">{label} evidence</p>
                <p class="text-text-secondary">{text}</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

